// TEST: GroupBy GroupKeys Aliased (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3017
// ERROR-CONTAINS: as another binding's value
// A `group_keys` result is NAME-KEYED, not a value. Codegen puts the whole
// CSR structure in locals suffixed off the BINDING name -- `gk__ngroups`,
// `gk__offsets`, `gk__perm` -- and hands the binding itself an opaque
// sentinel, so `group_by` recovers the grouping only by re-deriving those
// suffixed symbols from the name its grouping argument resolves to.
//
// `let gk2 = gk` therefore had nothing to bind: the alias carried the
// sentinel but no state, and the emitted C++ named `gk2__ngroups` /
// `gk2__offsets` / `gk2__perm`, none of which were ever declared. The program
// typechecked, lowered, emitted, and died in g++.
//
// The invariant was already ASSUMED elsewhere -- the grouped co-iteration
// check (026) decides "same keys" by comparing the gk operands' binding
// NAMES, which is only meaningful if a gk always IS its binding name. This is
// the same rule, enforced at its source instead.
let region = [0, 1, 0, 0, 1]
let v = [10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0, 50.0]
let gk = group_keys(region)
let gk2 = gk
let gv = group_by(v, gk2)
let s = method_for(gv) <@> lambda(r: Array<Float64 like RaggedIdx<_>>) -> reduce(r, (+)) |> compute
// EXPECT: BL3017 -- the alias `gk2` cannot carry the grouping. Pass the
// original `gk` to `group_by`; one binding may feed any number of group_by
// calls, which is exactly how grouped co-iteration is spelled.
